1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,075
There are only four kinds of wrong
answers on SAT table and graph questions.

2
00:00:04,397 --> 00:00:06,290
The wrong row or column.

3
00:00:06,861 --> 00:00:09,360
A comparison that runs the wrong way.

4
00:00:09,539 --> 00:00:12,467
A claim about every row
when one row disagrees.

5
00:00:12,682 --> 00:00:14,591
And the fourth one looks completely right,

6
00:00:14,611 --> 00:00:16,754
because every number in it is accurate.

7
00:00:16,818 --> 00:00:20,531
The first three are quick to catch,
and you'll see each one in action.

8
00:00:21,032 --> 00:00:24,389
The fourth is accurate data
that proves the wrong point,

9
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:27,853
like the highest value when the
claim is about the biggest change.

10
00:00:28,175 --> 00:00:30,476
That's question six, the hardest one,

11
00:00:30,496 --> 00:00:31,711
so stay for it.

12
00:00:31,732 --> 00:00:35,946
One habit handles all four: read
the claim before you read the table.

13
00:00:36,053 --> 00:00:39,018
Six SAT-style questions
with tables and graphs.

14
00:00:39,268 --> 00:00:41,945
Keep score as you go, and
see if you can get all six.

15
00:00:41,977 --> 00:00:45,029
The College Board calls this
skill Command of Evidence,

16
00:00:45,049 --> 00:00:46,227
quantitative.

17
00:00:46,263 --> 00:00:48,672
It's part of the Information
and Ideas domain,

18
00:00:48,692 --> 00:00:51,171
which is about a quarter of the
Reading and Writing section.

19
00:00:51,191 --> 00:00:53,528
You'll see a short passage and a table,

20
00:00:53,548 --> 00:00:55,191
a bar graph, or a line graph.

21
00:00:55,405 --> 00:00:57,727
The passage ends with a blank or a claim,

22
00:00:58,048 --> 00:01:00,191
and the question asks something like this:

23
00:01:00,547 --> 00:01:03,670
which choice most effectively uses
data from the table to complete the

24
00:01:03,690 --> 00:01:04,348
statement?

25
00:01:04,368 --> 00:01:06,418
Some versions ask you
to complete an example,

26
00:01:06,438 --> 00:01:08,439
or to support someone's conclusion.

27
00:01:08,832 --> 00:01:11,169
The job is the same every time.

28
00:01:11,189 --> 00:01:13,046
The right answer has to pass two tests.

29
00:01:13,601 --> 00:01:15,458
Test one: is it accurate?

30
00:01:16,387 --> 00:01:19,744
Every number and every comparison
has to match the graphic.

31
00:01:20,458 --> 00:01:22,795
Test two: is it relevant?

32
00:01:22,815 --> 00:01:25,494
It has to show exactly
what the sentence claims.

33
00:01:25,887 --> 00:01:29,458
Many wrong answers pass
test one and fail test two.

34
00:01:29,504 --> 00:01:31,698
Here's the method.

35
00:01:31,718 --> 00:01:34,004
Step one: find the claim.

36
00:01:34,289 --> 00:01:37,754
Read the sentence with the blank
and pin down exactly what it needs.

37
00:01:37,896 --> 00:01:38,840
Which group?

38
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:39,931
Which year?

39
00:01:40,147 --> 00:01:42,539
More, less, or a change over time?

40
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:45,456
Step two: find exactly those data.

41
00:01:45,814 --> 00:01:50,222
Go to the one row, column, bar,
or point the claim is about.

42
00:01:50,242 --> 00:01:51,886
You can ignore the rest of the graphic.

43
00:01:51,958 --> 00:01:55,100
Step three: check every
number in the choice.

44
00:01:55,673 --> 00:01:58,601
Right row, right column, right direction.

45
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:01,581
If one number or one comparison is off,

46
00:02:01,601 --> 00:02:03,244
the whole choice is wrong.

47
00:02:03,304 --> 00:02:04,446
Question one.

48
00:02:04,804 --> 00:02:08,712
A school librarian recorded the number
of print books and e-books that students

49
00:02:08,732 --> 00:02:11,232
in each grade checked
out during one semester.

50
00:02:11,732 --> 00:02:14,375
Ninth graders checked out
more print books than e-books,

51
00:02:14,625 --> 00:02:16,804
but twelfth graders showed
the opposite pattern:

52
00:02:17,018 --> 00:02:17,785
blank

53
00:02:17,805 --> 00:02:20,784
Which choice most effectively uses
data from the table to complete the

54
00:02:20,804 --> 00:02:21,462
statement?

55
00:02:21,482 --> 00:02:22,660
Pause and try it.

56
00:02:25,903 --> 00:02:27,705
Step one: find the claim.

57
00:02:27,725 --> 00:02:31,439
The blank is about twelfth graders,
and it says opposite pattern.

58
00:02:31,975 --> 00:02:34,046
Ninth graders checked
out more print books.

59
00:02:34,224 --> 00:02:37,475
So twelfth graders must have checked
out more e-books than print books.

60
00:02:37,576 --> 00:02:40,718
Step two: go to the twelfth grade row.

61
00:02:40,861 --> 00:02:44,504
Two hundred ten print books,
three hundred fifty-six e-books.

62
00:02:44,932 --> 00:02:45,699
More e-books.

63
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:49,326
Step three: check choice A.

64
00:02:50,075 --> 00:02:52,520
Twelfth graders, three
hundred fifty-six e-books,

65
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:54,091
two hundred ten print books.

66
00:02:54,111 --> 00:02:55,306
Every number matches.

67
00:02:55,326 --> 00:02:56,426
The answer is A.

68
00:02:56,446 --> 00:02:57,839
Now the wrong ones.

69
00:02:58,303 --> 00:03:01,374
C is the right row, but
it swaps the columns.

70
00:03:01,767 --> 00:03:04,532
D is accurate, but it's eleventh grade.

71
00:03:04,552 --> 00:03:08,017
That's trap number one:
the wrong row or column.

72
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:11,624
And B is accurate too, but
it's about ninth graders.

73
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:13,212
That's the first half of the sentence,

74
00:03:13,232 --> 00:03:14,183
not the blank.

75
00:03:14,203 --> 00:03:15,417
Question two.

76
00:03:15,952 --> 00:03:20,040
Students in a psychology class asked
volunteers to study a list of thirty words

77
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:23,611
in one of three conditions: in silence,

78
00:03:23,631 --> 00:03:27,060
with instrumental music, or
with music that had lyrics.

79
00:03:27,096 --> 00:03:30,862
The students concluded that music with
lyrics interfered with recall more than

80
00:03:30,882 --> 00:03:32,377
instrumental music did.

81
00:03:32,397 --> 00:03:35,841
Which choice most effectively uses data
from the graph to support the students'

82
00:03:35,861 --> 00:03:36,754
conclusion?

83
00:03:36,774 --> 00:03:37,952
Pause and try it.

84
00:03:42,195 --> 00:03:44,124
Here's what makes this one tricky.

85
00:03:44,552 --> 00:03:46,302
All four choices are true.

86
00:03:46,838 --> 00:03:49,032
Every one of them matches the graph.

87
00:03:49,052 --> 00:03:50,908
So test one won't help you here.

88
00:03:51,231 --> 00:03:52,683
Only test two will.

89
00:03:52,703 --> 00:03:54,167
So go back to the claim.

90
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:58,167
Lyrics interfered more
than instrumental music.

91
00:03:58,489 --> 00:04:00,826
That's a comparison
between two specific bars:

92
00:04:00,846 --> 00:04:02,703
lyrics and instrumental.

93
00:04:03,382 --> 00:04:05,489
Silence isn't part of the claim at all.

94
00:04:05,521 --> 00:04:08,093
Choice B compares exactly those two bars:

95
00:04:08,307 --> 00:04:10,287
thirteen point two words with lyrics,

96
00:04:10,307 --> 00:04:13,199
against seventeen point nine
with instrumental music.

97
00:04:13,593 --> 00:04:15,378
Lower recall with lyrics.

98
00:04:15,699 --> 00:04:18,842
The answer is B.

99
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:21,022
A and D compare music to silence.

100
00:04:21,378 --> 00:04:23,879
C compares instrumental music to silence.

101
00:04:24,272 --> 00:04:26,664
All true, all about the wrong bars.

102
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:29,164
That's trap number two:
true but irrelevant.

103
00:04:29,735 --> 00:04:31,771
Watch for it on every data question.

104
00:04:31,843 --> 00:04:33,165
Question three.

105
00:04:33,558 --> 00:04:37,430
A city's transit office tracked the
average number of weekday riders on two bus

106
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:39,879
routes from twenty nineteen
to twenty twenty-four.

107
00:04:40,129 --> 00:04:44,057
In twenty nineteen, Route Four
was the busier of the two routes.

108
00:04:44,342 --> 00:04:47,415
By twenty twenty-four,
however, that had changed:

109
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:48,357
blank

110
00:04:48,377 --> 00:04:52,357
Same kind of question: which choice
uses data from the graph to complete the

111
00:04:52,377 --> 00:04:52,630
statement?

112
00:04:52,650 --> 00:04:53,828
Pause and try it.

113
00:04:58,071 --> 00:05:02,035
The claim: by twenty
twenty-four, things had changed.

114
00:05:02,285 --> 00:05:04,821
Route Four was busier in twenty nineteen,

115
00:05:04,857 --> 00:05:07,285
so now Route Nine has to be busier.

116
00:05:07,427 --> 00:05:08,749
Go to twenty twenty-four.

117
00:05:09,213 --> 00:05:11,535
Route Nine is at about fifty-six hundred.

118
00:05:11,963 --> 00:05:14,142
Route Four is at about forty-five hundred.

119
00:05:14,163 --> 00:05:18,643
So Route Four had about eleven
hundred fewer riders than Route Nine.

120
00:05:18,663 --> 00:05:19,877
That's choice A.

121
00:05:19,947 --> 00:05:20,391
Now look at B.

122
00:05:20,411 --> 00:05:23,283
Same number, same year.

123
00:05:23,303 --> 00:05:27,125
But it says Route Nine had eleven
hundred fewer riders than Route Four.

124
00:05:27,625 --> 00:05:28,768
That's backward.

125
00:05:29,375 --> 00:05:32,590
Trap number three: a comparison
in the wrong direction.

126
00:05:32,768 --> 00:05:34,998
When two choices share the same numbers,

127
00:05:35,018 --> 00:05:36,983
check which way the comparison runs.

128
00:05:37,024 --> 00:05:40,218
C is true, but it compares
each route to twenty twenty,

129
00:05:40,238 --> 00:05:41,381
not to each other.

130
00:05:41,881 --> 00:05:45,504
And D gives Route Nine's
number without Route Four's,

131
00:05:45,524 --> 00:05:47,845
so it can't show which route was busier.

132
00:05:47,894 --> 00:05:49,073
Question four.

133
00:05:49,537 --> 00:05:53,001
A team of foresters planted
tree seedlings at four sites.

134
00:05:53,108 --> 00:05:56,588
At each site, half of the seedlings were
watered once a week during their first

135
00:05:56,608 --> 00:05:59,143
summer, and the other
half were not watered.

136
00:05:59,287 --> 00:06:02,945
The foresters concluded that weekly
watering improved survival at most,

137
00:06:02,965 --> 00:06:04,608
but not all, of the sites.

138
00:06:04,670 --> 00:06:08,614
Which choice most effectively uses data
from the table to support the foresters'

139
00:06:08,634 --> 00:06:09,556
conclusion?

140
00:06:09,576 --> 00:06:10,755
Pause and try it.

141
00:06:14,998 --> 00:06:16,426
The claim has two parts.

142
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:19,212
Watering helped at most sites.

143
00:06:19,783 --> 00:06:21,462
And it didn't help at all of them.

144
00:06:21,498 --> 00:06:26,048
So the right answer needs both: the
sites where watered seedlings did better,

145
00:06:26,068 --> 00:06:27,533
and a site where they didn't.

146
00:06:27,591 --> 00:06:28,000
Check each row.

147
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:31,770
North: eighty-two beats sixty-four.

148
00:06:32,198 --> 00:06:34,143
East: seventy-five beats seventy.

149
00:06:34,163 --> 00:06:35,947
South: ninety beats fifty-eight.

150
00:06:36,126 --> 00:06:38,305
West: sixty-eight is
less than seventy-one.

151
00:06:39,305 --> 00:06:41,535
Three sites yes, one site no.

152
00:06:41,555 --> 00:06:43,484
Choice C says exactly that.

153
00:06:43,541 --> 00:06:46,970
A says watered seedlings
did better at every site.

154
00:06:47,863 --> 00:06:49,613
The West row says otherwise.

155
00:06:49,791 --> 00:06:52,862
That's trap number four:
overgeneralization.

156
00:06:53,576 --> 00:06:57,934
Words like every, all, and always
need every single row to agree.

157
00:06:57,963 --> 00:07:02,427
B is true, but it's one number
with nothing to compare it to.

158
00:07:02,463 --> 00:07:04,963
And D gives only the West site.

159
00:07:05,319 --> 00:07:07,963
It shows the not all part,
but not the most part.

160
00:07:08,034 --> 00:07:09,606
Half of a claim isn't enough.

161
00:07:09,698 --> 00:07:11,198
So those are the four traps.

162
00:07:11,769 --> 00:07:13,376
Wrong row or column.

163
00:07:13,948 --> 00:07:15,071
True but irrelevant.

164
00:07:15,091 --> 00:07:16,412
Wrong direction.

165
00:07:16,627 --> 00:07:18,519
And overgeneralization.

166
00:07:19,627 --> 00:07:22,142
On harder questions, they don't go away.

167
00:07:22,162 --> 00:07:23,519
They just get harder to see.

168
00:07:24,305 --> 00:07:25,124
Here are two.

169
00:07:25,144 --> 00:07:26,465
Question five.

170
00:07:27,001 --> 00:07:30,909
Researchers surveyed all the volunteers
at three nature centers about whether

171
00:07:30,929 --> 00:07:34,251
they had ever submitted a bird
sighting to an online database.

172
00:07:34,929 --> 00:07:37,124
Center Two had the fewest volunteers,

173
00:07:37,144 --> 00:07:40,144
but the researchers found that it had
the highest rate of participation:

174
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:41,055
blank

175
00:07:41,075 --> 00:07:42,254
Pause and try it.

176
00:07:46,496 --> 00:07:47,496
Find the claim.

177
00:07:48,818 --> 00:07:52,119
It's not about the most volunteers
who submitted a sighting.

178
00:07:52,139 --> 00:07:53,995
It's about the highest rate.

179
00:07:55,781 --> 00:07:57,246
A rate is a percent.

180
00:07:57,638 --> 00:08:00,389
So the only column that
matters is the last one.

181
00:08:00,436 --> 00:08:02,186
Center Two: sixty percent.

182
00:08:02,722 --> 00:08:04,579
Center One: forty.

183
00:08:05,150 --> 00:08:07,008
Center Three: thirty-five.

184
00:08:07,685 --> 00:08:11,238
Choice D gives all three, and
Center Two is the highest.

185
00:08:11,258 --> 00:08:12,363
That's the answer.

186
00:08:12,383 --> 00:08:13,883
The trap is A.

187
00:08:15,097 --> 00:08:18,577
Center Three had one hundred five
volunteers who submitted a sighting,

188
00:08:18,597 --> 00:08:20,275
the biggest number in that column.

189
00:08:20,632 --> 00:08:22,776
True, but that's a count, not a rate.

190
00:08:23,026 --> 00:08:24,061
Wrong column.

191
00:08:24,203 --> 00:08:26,863
B only shows that Center
Two was the smallest.

192
00:08:26,883 --> 00:08:29,383
And C leaves Center Two out completely.

193
00:08:29,456 --> 00:08:32,705
Last one, and it's the hardest.

194
00:08:33,170 --> 00:08:37,186
Researchers compared the average July
surface temperatures of three lakes in

195
00:08:37,206 --> 00:08:39,365
nineteen ninety-five and twenty twenty.

196
00:08:39,385 --> 00:08:42,865
All three lakes warmed,
but not by the same amount.

197
00:08:42,885 --> 00:08:47,615
In fact, the lake that warmed the most
was the one that had been the coolest in

198
00:08:47,635 --> 00:08:50,010
nineteen ninety-five: blank

199
00:08:50,035 --> 00:08:51,051
Pause and try it.

200
00:08:51,071 --> 00:08:52,000
Take a little longer on this one.

201
00:08:57,193 --> 00:08:57,887
Find the claim.

202
00:08:57,907 --> 00:08:59,157
It has two parts.

203
00:08:59,836 --> 00:09:02,621
Which lake was the coolest
in nineteen ninety-five?

204
00:09:02,871 --> 00:09:04,764
And which lake warmed the most?

205
00:09:05,978 --> 00:09:07,978
Warmed the most is a change.

206
00:09:08,335 --> 00:09:10,316
So you're not looking for the tallest bar.

207
00:09:10,336 --> 00:09:12,943
You're looking for the
biggest gap between bars.

208
00:09:13,001 --> 00:09:15,266
The coolest in nineteen
ninety-five was Lake Q,

209
00:09:15,286 --> 00:09:17,501
at eighteen point five degrees Celsius.

210
00:09:18,286 --> 00:09:19,572
Now the changes.

211
00:09:20,250 --> 00:09:22,250
Lake P went up one degree.

212
00:09:22,394 --> 00:09:24,445
Lake Q went up two and a half.

213
00:09:24,465 --> 00:09:26,394
Lake R went up half a degree.

214
00:09:26,929 --> 00:09:30,552
Lake Q warmed the most, and
choice C says exactly that,

215
00:09:30,572 --> 00:09:31,965
with every number right.

216
00:09:32,011 --> 00:09:33,439
The trap is A.

217
00:09:34,117 --> 00:09:37,047
Lake R has the tallest bars in both years.

218
00:09:37,261 --> 00:09:38,904
It's the warmest lake.

219
00:09:39,153 --> 00:09:41,868
But the warmest lake isn't
the lake that warmed the most.

220
00:09:42,654 --> 00:09:45,205
That's true but irrelevant
in its hardest form:

221
00:09:45,225 --> 00:09:47,939
the highest value instead
of the biggest change.

222
00:09:48,002 --> 00:09:50,930
B is true, but it leaves out Lake Q.

223
00:09:51,645 --> 00:09:53,018
And D gets half the claim.

224
00:09:53,038 --> 00:09:57,608
Lake Q was still the
coolest in twenty twenty.

225
00:09:57,930 --> 00:10:01,180
But D never says that
Lake Q warmed the most.

226
00:10:01,246 --> 00:10:03,711
Here's the whole method in one place.

227
00:10:03,745 --> 00:10:07,818
First, read the sentence with the
blank before you study the graphic.

228
00:10:07,961 --> 00:10:10,905
Mark exactly what it needs: which group,

229
00:10:10,925 --> 00:10:13,317
which time, and what kind of comparison.

230
00:10:13,342 --> 00:10:15,914
Second, go only to those data.

231
00:10:16,663 --> 00:10:19,414
One row, one column, one pair of bars.

232
00:10:19,842 --> 00:10:22,572
If the claim says rate, use the percent.

233
00:10:22,592 --> 00:10:24,591
If it says change, subtract.

234
00:10:24,613 --> 00:10:29,006
Third, check every number and every
comparison word in the choice.

235
00:10:29,435 --> 00:10:30,649
More or fewer.

236
00:10:30,863 --> 00:10:32,112
Higher or lower.

237
00:10:32,685 --> 00:10:34,649
Every, most, or some.

238
00:10:36,042 --> 00:10:40,935
Then ask one last question: does
this choice prove the claim,

239
00:10:41,577 --> 00:10:42,827
or is it just true?

240
00:10:42,904 --> 00:10:43,940
One more tip.

241
00:10:44,547 --> 00:10:47,726
Don't try to understand the whole
graphic before you read the claim.

242
00:10:48,154 --> 00:10:50,813
Most of the data on the screen
won't matter for the claim,

243
00:10:50,833 --> 00:10:54,547
and that extra data is exactly
what the wrong answers like to use.

244
00:10:54,663 --> 00:10:55,663
Recap.

245
00:10:55,699 --> 00:10:57,163
Read the claim first.

246
00:10:57,198 --> 00:10:58,984
Find exactly those data.

247
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:00,321
Check every number.

248
00:11:00,341 --> 00:11:04,234
And watch for the four
traps: wrong row or column,

249
00:11:04,341 --> 00:11:07,234
true but irrelevant, wrong direction,

250
00:11:07,270 --> 00:11:08,913
and overgeneralization.

251
00:11:08,946 --> 00:11:11,712
All six questions in this video
were written for this video,

252
00:11:11,732 --> 00:11:14,839
in the style of the real test,
and the data are made up.

253
00:11:15,053 --> 00:11:19,212
For official practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter by

254
00:11:19,232 --> 00:11:21,589
skill, including Command of Evidence.

255
00:11:21,633 --> 00:11:23,632
If this helped, subscribe.

256
00:11:25,561 --> 00:11:28,719
The next video covers another
skill from this same domain:

